license: MIT
keywords:
  - joint-embedding predictive architecture
  - identifiability
  - self-supervised learning
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
abstract: >-
  Empirically maps how and where the Klindt–LeCun–Balestriero
  Gaussian-identifiability theorem for Joint-Embedding Predictive
  Architectures breaks as the latent regime leaves the theorem's
  Gaussian boundary. A ladder of procedurally generated worlds with
  known ground-truth latents (continuous Gaussian, discretized
  Gaussian, Markov-chain categories, a rule-governed grammar, and a
  Turkish orthography caret-rule lexicon) is trained under four
  JEPA-family objectives with a frozen, pre-registered protocol
  (append-only amendments A1–A6), reporting all outcomes including
  honest nulls.
